WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:02.682
A biodiversity hotspot isn't

2
00:00:02.682 --> 00:00:04.506
just rich in species.

3
00:00:04.832 --> 00:00:06.996
It's defined by what it has lost.

4
00:00:07.368 --> 00:00:09.653
To qualify, a region needs

5
00:00:09.653 --> 00:00:12.114
at least 1,500 plant species

6
00:00:12.114 --> 00:00:13.784
found nowhere else,

7
00:00:14.090 --> 00:00:15.896
and must have lost at least

8
00:00:15.896 --> 00:00:17.636
70 percent of its original

9
00:00:17.636 --> 00:00:18.372
vegetation.

10
00:00:18.897 --> 00:00:20.525
There are 36 worldwide.

11
00:00:21.020 --> 00:00:22.825
Four reach into India:

12
00:00:23.061 --> 00:00:24.976
the Himalaya, Indo-Burma,

13
00:00:24.976 --> 00:00:26.354
the Western Ghats,

14
00:00:26.669 --> 00:00:28.430
and Sundaland, through the

15
00:00:28.430 --> 00:00:29.513
Nicobar Islands.

16
00:00:29.727 --> 00:00:31.955
But a hotspot is a conservation

17
00:00:31.955 --> 00:00:33.177
label, not a law.

18
00:00:33.468 --> 00:00:35.263
Legal protection comes from

19
00:00:35.263 --> 00:00:37.191
the Wild Life Protection Act:

20
00:00:37.674 --> 00:00:39.760
national parks, sanctuaries,

21
00:00:39.760 --> 00:00:41.622
conservation reserves and

22
00:00:41.622 --> 00:00:43.037
community reserves.

23
00:00:43.293 --> 00:00:44.912
In a national park, grazing

24
00:00:44.912 --> 00:00:45.811
is not allowed.

25
00:00:46.217 --> 00:00:48.046
In a sanctuary, the Chief

26
00:00:48.046 --> 00:00:49.947
Wildlife Warden can permit

27
00:00:49.947 --> 00:00:50.825
some rights.

28
00:00:51.077 --> 00:00:52.509
Rich, and threatened,

29
00:00:52.866 --> 00:00:53.769
by definition.

30
00:00:54.148 --> 00:00:54.599
Your turn.

31
00:00:54.964 --> 00:00:56.426
Which hotspot covers the

32
00:00:56.426 --> 00:00:57.400
Nicobar Islands?
